1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,535
Chai Corner is busier than ever.

2
00:00:02,835 --> 00:00:05,889
But one morning, Kabir's
cash counter breaks down.

3
00:00:06,439 --> 00:00:09,359
Customers can't pay, so
the line stops moving.

4
00:00:09,659 --> 00:00:11,291
Asha's chai goes cold.

5
00:00:11,291 --> 00:00:13,473
Meena runs out of clean cups.

6
00:00:13,773 --> 00:00:17,012
One small part fails, and
the whole stall suffers.

7
00:00:17,662 --> 00:00:20,351
Chai Corner is not six separate jobs.

8
00:00:20,351 --> 00:00:21,782
It is one system.

9
00:00:22,482 --> 00:00:24,687
This idea came from biology.

10
00:00:24,687 --> 00:00:29,662
Ludwig von Bertalanffy, an Austrian
biologist, studied living things as

11
00:00:29,662 --> 00:00:30,562
systems.

12
00:00:30,815 --> 00:00:34,719
In your body, the heart, lungs
and brain all need each other.

13
00:00:34,719 --> 00:00:36,098
No part works alone.

14
00:00:36,448 --> 00:00:41,746
He called it general system theory,
and wrote about it from the 1940s.

15
00:00:42,096 --> 00:00:47,663
In 1966, Daniel Katz and Robert Kahn
applied it to organisations, in a

16
00:00:47,663 --> 00:00:48,855
famous book.

17
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:51,597
So, what is the systems approach?

18
00:00:51,897 --> 00:00:56,897
It sees an organisation as one system
of connected parts, all working toward

19
00:00:56,897 --> 00:00:57,797
one goal.

20
00:00:58,114 --> 00:01:00,245
Each part is called a subsystem.

21
00:01:00,245 --> 00:01:03,274
That means a smaller
system inside the big one.

22
00:01:03,624 --> 00:01:08,514
At Chai Corner, the kitchen, the cash
counter and the cleaning are subsystems.

23
00:01:08,514 --> 00:01:10,172
Ravi links them together.

24
00:01:10,822 --> 00:01:13,187
Every system runs on the same loop.

25
00:01:13,187 --> 00:01:14,789
Let's watch one morning.

26
00:01:15,139 --> 00:01:16,039
Inputs.

27
00:01:15,797 --> 00:01:16,874
What goes in.

28
00:01:16,874 --> 00:01:19,935
Milk, tea leaves, sugar,
and people's time.

29
00:01:20,285 --> 00:01:21,185
Process.

30
00:01:21,027 --> 00:01:23,337
Asha turns the inputs into chai.

31
00:01:23,337 --> 00:01:25,416
This is called transformation.

32
00:01:25,766 --> 00:01:26,666
Outputs.

33
00:01:26,511 --> 00:01:27,748
What comes out.

34
00:01:27,748 --> 00:01:30,170
Hot chai, and customers who pay.

35
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:31,420
Feedback.

36
00:01:31,266 --> 00:01:33,688
Customers say the chai is too sweet.

37
00:01:34,038 --> 00:01:36,402
So next morning, Asha adds less sugar.

38
00:01:36,402 --> 00:01:39,723
What comes out, comes back,
and improves what goes in.

39
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:42,743
Chai Corner is also an open system.

40
00:01:42,743 --> 00:01:45,222
It is linked to the world around it.

41
00:01:45,572 --> 00:01:49,575
Customers, suppliers, the government,
and competitors all push on it.

42
00:01:49,925 --> 00:01:52,626
On a festival evening, the queue doubles.

43
00:01:52,626 --> 00:01:56,074
Ravi moves Kabir to serving,
and calls in extra help.

44
00:01:56,424 --> 00:01:59,457
A closed system has no
link to the outside.

45
00:01:59,457 --> 00:02:01,944
Real businesses never work that way.

46
00:02:02,594 --> 00:02:03,861
One more big word.

47
00:02:03,861 --> 00:02:04,761
Synergy.

48
00:02:04,858 --> 00:02:08,158
It means the whole is greater
than the sum of its parts.

49
00:02:08,508 --> 00:02:11,798
Working alone, six people
serve sixty cups an hour.

50
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:14,160
As one team, they serve a hundred.

51
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:17,894
So what does systems thinking
look like at work today?

52
00:02:18,244 --> 00:02:19,159
Subsystems.

53
00:02:19,159 --> 00:02:21,891
Every department is part of the whole.

54
00:02:21,891 --> 00:02:25,208
A hospital has emergency,
surgery and pharmacy.

55
00:02:25,558 --> 00:02:26,715
Interdependence.

56
00:02:26,715 --> 00:02:28,100
One delay spreads.

57
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,642
If a parts supplier is
late, a car factory stops.

58
00:02:31,992 --> 00:02:32,892
Feedback.

59
00:02:32,757 --> 00:02:34,145
Learn from results.

60
00:02:34,145 --> 00:02:38,180
Apps read user ratings, and fix
problems in the next update.

61
00:02:38,530 --> 00:02:39,525
Open system.

62
00:02:39,525 --> 00:02:41,260
Watch the world outside.

63
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:44,104
Shops stock up before the festival season.

64
00:02:44,454 --> 00:02:45,354
Synergy.

65
00:02:45,242 --> 00:02:47,008
Teams beat solo stars.

66
00:02:47,008 --> 00:02:51,385
Doctors, nurses and therapists
plan a patient's care together.

67
00:02:52,035 --> 00:02:54,540
So, is the systems approach enough?

68
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:55,691
Not by itself.

69
00:02:56,041 --> 00:02:57,094
The good side.

70
00:02:57,094 --> 00:03:01,074
It shows the big picture, and how
every part affects the others.

71
00:03:01,424 --> 00:03:02,512
The hard side.

72
00:03:02,512 --> 00:03:04,244
It can feel too abstract.

73
00:03:04,244 --> 00:03:07,659
It says everything is
connected, but not what to do.

74
00:03:08,009 --> 00:03:11,987
It gives managers a way to
see, not a step by step recipe.

75
00:03:12,337 --> 00:03:15,898
So the lesson is: see the whole
system, then fix the right part.

76
00:03:16,548 --> 00:03:17,529
Quick check.

77
00:03:17,529 --> 00:03:21,466
Ravi reads what customers say,
and changes tomorrow's recipe.

78
00:03:21,466 --> 00:03:23,813
Which part of the system is this?

79
00:03:25,713 --> 00:03:26,613
Feedback!

80
00:03:26,465 --> 00:03:30,568
Information from the output comes
back, and improves the system.

81
00:03:31,168 --> 00:03:32,177
So, remember.

82
00:03:32,477 --> 00:03:37,548
An organisation is one system, made
of connected parts called subsystems.

83
00:03:37,898 --> 00:03:42,357
Every system runs on a loop: inputs,
process, outputs, and feedback.

84
00:03:42,707 --> 00:03:46,487
It is open to the world, and the
whole is greater than its parts.

85
00:03:47,087 --> 00:03:52,000
Next time, we'll see why there is no one
best way to manage, in the contingency

86
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:52,900
approach.
